another question in the interim.Let's get to&nbsp; some of these Aha questions. Will China provide&nbsp;&nbsp; Iran with intelligence and continue to sell them&nbsp; weapons? Absolutely, yes.Bahrain is buzzing with&nbsp;&nbsp; revolution talk. Is it likely that a full-scale&nbsp; uprising will occur? Historically Bahrain had&nbsp;&nbsp; been between two-thirds and three-quarters Shia&nbsp; Muslim. The royal family is Sunni Muslim. When I&nbsp;&nbsp; was there during what is now known as the first&nbsp; Intifada—it was freaking hideous.So what the&nbsp;&nbsp; Bahraini royal family did in the intervening&nbsp; 30 years is they imported Syrians—mostly Sunni&nbsp;&nbsp; Muslims—gave them Bahraini citizenship and then&nbsp; started naturalizing Indian and Pakistani Muslims.&nbsp;&nbsp; So now it's closer to 50-50. But the Sunnis still&nbsp; have money, the Shia are still poor. So there is&nbsp;&nbsp; always a threat of an uprising in Bahrain.Let's&nbsp; do that spot.You've probably noticed Rumble&nbsp;&nbsp; is growing fast and it's not slowing down. They're&nbsp; building a real alternative to big tech that puts&nbsp;&nbsp; creators first and actually protects free speech.&nbsp; And now there's Rumble Premium, an easy way to&nbsp;&nbsp; upgrade your experience. With Premium, you get&nbsp; ad-free viewing across the platform—no pre-rolls,&nbsp;&nbsp; no interruptions—just the content you came for.&nbsp; Plus, Premium members unlock exclusive content&nbsp;&nbsp; like bonus videos, behind-the-scenes drops, and&nbsp; more from your favorite creators like John and&nbsp;&nbsp; myself.Right now, Rumble is offering $10 off an&nbsp; annual subscription. Just go to rumble.com/premium&nbsp;&nbsp; and use the promo code studio at checkout. That's&nbsp; rumble.com/premium—promo code studio. No ads,&nbsp;&nbsp; freedom, and content you actually care about.&nbsp; That's the deal.Robby—just to remind—please&nbsp;&nbsp; remind people why they should consider migrating&nbsp; from YouTube over to Rumble.Yes. A few different&nbsp;&nbsp; reasons. From a purely monetary standpoint—Rumble&nbsp; pays a hell of a lot better. YouTube pays off of&nbsp;&nbsp; your ads. Rumble is paid off of watch hours.&nbsp; So basically for every nickel that they make&nbsp;&nbsp; on YouTube, they make about 95, 96 cents over on&nbsp; Rumble. So the money is definitely there.Also—if&nbsp;&nbsp; you are a channel subscriber on YouTube, YouTube&nbsp; takes a nice generous 40% cut off of your&nbsp;&nbsp; subscription fee. With Rumble, you subscribe for&nbsp; $5 a month and John and Ted keep every penny of&nbsp;&nbsp; it. Rumble doesn't take a dime for themselves.One&nbsp; of the listeners is asking Robby—what about the&nbsp;&nbsp; reputation Rumble has for being right-wing?&nbsp; Obviously I'm not right-wing. John's not&nbsp;&nbsp; right-wing.So let's just address that for a little&nbsp; bit. When last year YouTube demonetized John and&nbsp;&nbsp; Ted for almost three months—Rumble didn't. When&nbsp; the war with Ukraine started, YouTube deleted&nbsp;&nbsp; John's channel. YouTube shut down and throttled&nbsp; Ted Rall's channel. YouTube removed RT.So if&nbsp;&nbsp; you're going to use YouTube as your beacon of free&nbsp; speech, maybe you should consider moving to Israel&nbsp;&nbsp; because that's the kind of free speech that you&nbsp; want. If you want free speech, go to Rumble. We've&nbsp;&nbsp; never once heard from Rumble to say, please take&nbsp; this down. YouTube has been a lot of trouble for&nbsp;&nbsp; us.Lastly—when YouTube was paying a couple of&nbsp; hundred dollars for what John and Ted are doing,&nbsp;&nbsp; Rumble—just off of people watching—was allowing&nbsp; them to pay their bills. Not YouTube. Thank you&nbsp;&nbsp; very much, Robby.Just because someone's right-wing&nbsp; doesn't mean that they're your enemy. That's true.&nbsp;&nbsp; I can vouch for that. I got censored more by The&nbsp; Nation and Mother Jones than I ever did by Forbes&nbsp;&nbsp; or Fortune as a cartoonist.Slay123—thanks for&nbsp; the 10 bucks. Did you guys catch the president's&nbsp;&nbsp; war briefing? Beautiful. Oh my God—this is like&nbsp; moving. Beautiful curtains and the construction&nbsp;&nbsp; in his new ballroom. At this point, Idiocracy&nbsp; feels less like satire and more of a documentary.&nbsp;&nbsp; What the [ __ ]?You know, it's the gold leaf that&nbsp; I just don't get. There's gold leaf everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp; It's going to all have to be taken down. It's&nbsp; a waste of time and money. It's like the decor&nbsp;&nbsp; is early Dolomite.Do we think Gulf countries want&nbsp; the U.S. to rebuild bases that have been damaged?&nbsp;&nbsp; From Breed. Thanks for the 4.99.That's a good&nbsp; question. The damage hasn't been very extensive&nbsp;&nbsp; so far. Although there's breaking news—Robby&nbsp; highlighted for us—that Qatar is now bombing&nbsp;&nbsp; Iran. A country of 250,000 people is bombing&nbsp; a country of 92 million people right now. The&nbsp;&nbsp; Qatari Navy has intercepted—15 minutes ago—a&nbsp; ship full of Iranian missiles and drones and&nbsp;&nbsp; is forcing it into harbor in Doha.Is the Qatari&nbsp; military good?It's very tiny, but it's trained&nbsp;&nbsp; and equipped by the United States. So it's good.&nbsp; The Qataris are playing hardball right now.It's&nbsp;&nbsp; been reported that Russia and Saudi&nbsp; Arabia spoke on Monday. Any significance&nbsp;&nbsp; to this?Yeah—the Saudis—this is Mohammed bin&nbsp; Salman. The guy is just the most unpredictable&nbsp;&nbsp; leader in Saudi history. You might as well keep&nbsp; the lines of communication open—maybe the Russians&nbsp;&nbsp; can say something to the Iranians while the Saudis&nbsp; say something to the Americans and we can all back&nbsp;&nbsp; off this thing.By the way—John, if you're okay&nbsp; with this—I'm focusing on the questions related&nbsp;&nbsp; to the war and prioritizing those.I've been seeing&nbsp; reports—unconfirmed—that Israel has been bombing&nbsp;&nbsp; oil infrastructure in Saudi Arabia and blaming it&nbsp; on Iran. Although you can see on radar incoming&nbsp;&nbsp; missiles. So I don't think the Israelis are&nbsp; bombing Saudi oil fields. They don't need to&nbsp;&nbsp; because the Iranians—for the last 40 years—have&nbsp; been targeting Saudi oil fields.Now that the war&nbsp;&nbsp; has started—is there any way to end it with&nbsp; peace in the region and without an increase&nbsp;&nbsp; in terror attacks? Not the second one.What will&nbsp; the impact be if Congress does take Trump's war&nbsp;&nbsp; powers away? It doesn't look like that's going&nbsp; to happen. There's going to be a vote. There are&nbsp;&nbsp; some conservatives—Thomas Massie, Rand Paul—who&nbsp; are going to vote for the War Powers Resolution.&nbsp;&nbsp; But there's also going to be the John Fettermans&nbsp; and other Democrats-in-name-only who are going to&nbsp;&nbsp; cross the other way. So it'll probably look kind&nbsp; of like a normal vote in Congress that Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp; try to get through that fails.And if it's written&nbsp; as a sense-of-the-Congress resolution—it's not&nbsp;&nbsp; going to have any teeth or authority anyway. If&nbsp; it's done as a bill, the president's going to&nbsp;&nbsp; veto it.Over the past year several current and&nbsp; former Israeli officials have pointed at Turkey&nbsp;&nbsp; as the next strategic threat to Israel. God,&nbsp; what a change. They used to be buddies.Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; they used to be close buddies—which is one of the&nbsp; reasons why the Greeks and the Palestinians were&nbsp;&nbsp; so close.You and I should explore this more deeply&nbsp; at some point. But let's say that the Israelis are&nbsp;&nbsp; successful in Iran, and the Iranian leadership&nbsp; is removed from power. There's no need to worry&nbsp;&nbsp; anymore about an Iranian nuclear program. But the&nbsp; Israelis are always looking to attack the next&nbsp;&nbsp; guy.Does Turkey constitute a meaningful military,&nbsp; diplomatic, or economic threat to Israel? In terms&nbsp;&nbsp; of capabilities—yes. Diplomatically—yes, I do&nbsp; believe the Israelis have their hands full with&nbsp;&nbsp; the Turks. But that's life. Maybe the Israelis&nbsp; should try to start getting along with people.The&nbsp;&nbsp; Turks aren't going to invade Israel.Have you&nbsp; seen that Israelis are going to advance troops&nbsp;&nbsp; into southern Lebanon? They already have. Greater&nbsp; Israel coming true. Although they do that all the&nbsp;&nbsp; time—so it's hard to say whether this is the&nbsp; time. There's this like 20-mile no-man's-land&nbsp;&nbsp; along the border.Do we think Hezbollah's&nbsp; intervention was a smart move for them? It&nbsp;&nbsp; does feel a little bit like they have nothing to&nbsp; lose. But remember—martyrdom is something to be&nbsp;&nbsp; aspired to.Big fan of the content you produce.&nbsp; Is there a reason why the UAE and Dubai is being&nbsp;&nbsp; disproportionately targeted compared to other Gulf&nbsp; countries by the count of missiles intercepted?&nbsp;&nbsp; Reports are showing UAE and Qatar running out of&nbsp; Patriot missiles. How do you think that will play&nbsp;&nbsp; out?The reports from last night were that the&nbsp; UAE would be out of Patriot missiles in seven&nbsp;&nbsp; days and the Qataris would be out of them in four&nbsp; days—and we can't resupply them quickly enough. So&nbsp;&nbsp; this is a problem.The reason why the UAE has been&nbsp; so disproportionately targeted is that the UAE has&nbsp;&nbsp; been at the forefront of the attacks against the&nbsp; Houthis. And despite the fact that Dubai is home&nbsp;&nbsp; to thousands of Iranians and Iranian businesses—I&nbsp; think the Iranian government just wants to bloody&nbsp;&nbsp; the Emiratis' noses as best they can.What do&nbsp; you make of Marco Rubio and Mike Johnson's&nbsp;&nbsp; suggestions that we attacked because Israel was&nbsp; going to attack Iran? What could possibly be the&nbsp;&nbsp; PR strategy of such statements from such pro-war&nbsp; and pro-Israel leaders? It's baffling. It's bad&nbsp;&nbsp; PR.John—why is the president still not speaking&nbsp; to the American people? When he announced the&nbsp;&nbsp; start of the attack—he did it at two o'clock in&nbsp; the morning on Truth Social. He did not address&nbsp;&nbsp; the American people. He did not stand at the&nbsp; podium in the East Room of the White House and&nbsp;&nbsp; make an announcement that essentially we're at&nbsp; war. He's only been going between Washington and&nbsp;&nbsp; Mar-a-Lago back and forth. He has not begun&nbsp; crisscrossing the country to explain why he&nbsp;&nbsp; decided to send young American men and women into&nbsp; battle. He owes that.The only thing I can think&nbsp;&nbsp; of is that he wants to do this as kind of a side&nbsp; project. He doesn't want it to become a defining&nbsp;&nbsp; feature of his presidency.Right. But you can't—it&nbsp; can't be. It's so embarrassing. This morning on&nbsp;&nbsp; NPR—they were playing clips of him promising,&nbsp; criticizing Obama for attacking Iran—saying&nbsp;&nbsp; I'll never get you into any wars. These wars&nbsp; are stupid. Why do we spend money on blood and&nbsp;&nbsp; treasure overseas—over and over. And then this.The&nbsp; American people aren't that sharp—but they're not&nbsp;&nbsp; that stupid either.No. And you've got to talk&nbsp; to the people. You've got to explain the policy.&nbsp;&nbsp; There's no effort here at all. Maybe they don't&nbsp; think they have a good story to tell.I saw a clip&nbsp;&nbsp; where they were interviewing Republican voters.&nbsp; Do you support or oppose the attack against&nbsp;&nbsp; Iran? A third of Republicans support it—two-thirds&nbsp; oppose it. And the supporters—why do you support&nbsp;&nbsp; it? These are very bad people. Iran's a bad&nbsp; country. There are a lot of bad countries&nbsp;&nbsp; out there. A lot. You're going to attack all of&nbsp; them? What are you—nuts?It's a good question. I've&nbsp;&nbsp; always wondered that. In the run-up to the war&nbsp; against Iraq—I used to compile all the lists of&nbsp;&nbsp; dictatorships that the U.S. supports or tolerates&nbsp; around the world. It's a long list.Ted—the&nbsp;&nbsp; markets are now open and they're all crashing&nbsp; and oil is skyrocketing. Looking at the Dow&nbsp;&nbsp; Jones Industrial Average is down over a thousand&nbsp; points right now—with the market having opened 14&nbsp;&nbsp; minutes ago—we're down 2.13%. This is a freefall.&nbsp; This is a crash. They're gonna have to shut down&nbsp;&nbsp; trading.Yeah—there's automatic triggers. So&nbsp; that probably will happen. If they shut down&nbsp;&nbsp; trading—that's gonna be a huge signal. This&nbsp; is something that the president can't ignore.&nbsp;&nbsp; He's getting calls right now from Fortune 500 CEOs&nbsp; flooding into the administration and saying you're&nbsp;&nbsp; [ __ ] up the economy. Donald Trump doesn't want&nbsp; to [ __ ] up the economy. That's the one thing he&nbsp;&nbsp; can't afford to do.But Ted—let me ask you—why in&nbsp; the world would gold and silver be crashing? Gold&nbsp;&nbsp; and silver should be going up.It should be going&nbsp; up—and gold's down three and a third percent—down&nbsp;&nbsp; a hundred and seventy-eight dollars and ninety&nbsp; cents.The only thing I can think of is that&nbsp;&nbsp; we're seeing some profit-taking because people&nbsp; who invest in precious metals traditionally do&nbsp;&nbsp; so over a longer period. It's not something that&nbsp; they trade in and out of in a day.That's true. I&nbsp;&nbsp; think you might have people who bought a few&nbsp; months ago and are thinking I'm going to get&nbsp;&nbsp; this right now while the market is high. What I&nbsp; would watch—if it keeps going down later in the&nbsp;&nbsp; day—that's really gonna be strange—but it could&nbsp; bounce back.Interestingly—the small-cap Russell&nbsp;&nbsp; 2000 index is up. That's good.Yeah—that's super&nbsp; interesting.Now it's dropping. Now it's falling.&nbsp;&nbsp; So we shall see what happens here.If you're&nbsp; the president—you're sitting there—you're&nbsp;&nbsp; watching that—you're getting this news. How is&nbsp; the administration going to respond to a stock&nbsp;&nbsp; market crash? Knowing Donald Trump—he is going to&nbsp; put on a tough face and say stay the course. This&nbsp;&nbsp; is the right thing to do. We're going to fight&nbsp; until we win. We're going to destroy the Iranians&nbsp;&nbsp; and their nuclear program and everybody's going&nbsp; to make money. So just stay the course.What are&nbsp;&nbsp; our thoughts on reports that MBS of Saudi Arabia&nbsp; encouraged Trump to attack Iran? We know that's&nbsp;&nbsp; true. Benefits from spreading such reports? Do&nbsp; they have a net positive or negative effect for&nbsp;&nbsp; MBS or Saudi Arabia?I gotta say negative.I would&nbsp; think negative too. It doesn't make sense to me&nbsp;&nbsp; because it was MBS's idea to try to normalize&nbsp; and improve relations between Saudi Arabia and&nbsp;&nbsp; Iran. So why attack? I just don't get it.I do&nbsp; want to take this question—even though it's not&nbsp;&nbsp; about Iran. Eastern Congo is experiencing one of&nbsp; the worst humanitarian crises on the planet right&nbsp;&nbsp; now—has been for decades. 80% of women there&nbsp; say they've been raped. There are U.S.-backed&nbsp;&nbsp; agreements—the critics say are designed&nbsp; to legally extract their resources without&nbsp;&nbsp; consent. We hear nothing about it—with one of&nbsp; the four people on earth being African within 50&nbsp;&nbsp; years. Powerful interests are already playing the&nbsp; long game over the continent while we look away.&nbsp;&nbsp; What's your specific position on U.S. policy&nbsp; toward the Democratic Republic of the Congo?&nbsp;&nbsp; Any mission in Africa in the past?One mission in&nbsp; Africa in the past—in Kenya—that I'm not permitted&nbsp;&nbsp; to talk about. And then when I was with the&nbsp; Senate Foreign Relations Committee—I did work in&nbsp;&nbsp; Djibouti, Ethiopia, and Somalia.The odd thing&nbsp; is U.S.-DRC relations are actually quite good.&nbsp;&nbsp; We buy as much of their rare earth metals as&nbsp; they could possibly dig out of the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp; We make everything from chrome bumpers to cell&nbsp; phones to guided missile systems with their rare&nbsp;&nbsp; earth metals. The government is very weak in the&nbsp; eastern part—very weak—versus Rwanda. Rwanda is&nbsp;&nbsp; like this big. DRC is huge. And the Rwandans just&nbsp; batter them about the face and head at will. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; chaos over there in the east. But this is one of&nbsp; those conflicts that Donald Trump took credit for&nbsp;&nbsp; solving. This was one of his arguments in support&nbsp; of the Nobel Peace Prize—that he brought peace to&nbsp;&nbsp; the eastern Congo. And the peace lasted a couple&nbsp; of weeks.Yeah. It's interesting because Rwanda and&nbsp;&nbsp; Congo were both Belgian colonies. So their&nbsp; colonial experience didn't differ that much.&nbsp;&nbsp; They were both raped by King Leopold.But why&nbsp; does nobody care? I think this is because it's&nbsp;&nbsp; very remote. It's hard to get to. If you're&nbsp; a reporter who's interested in Congo—no one&nbsp;&nbsp; will send you there. No one's interested. American&nbsp; media outlets give no shits about it—which is why&nbsp;&nbsp; the American people—it's completely off their&nbsp; radar. Which is why I watch BBC six o'clock&nbsp;&nbsp; every day on PBS.Beats and Meats. Thanks for the&nbsp; 9.99. Do you think Putin will threaten Israel with&nbsp;&nbsp; nukes if they threaten Iran with nukes? This is&nbsp; essentially what Professor Jiang hypothesized a&nbsp;&nbsp; few years ago in his infamous video "The Iran&nbsp; Trap."I don't think so. Honestly—I think the&nbsp;&nbsp; Russians are far more responsible on this issue&nbsp; than the Israelis are. I don't think they'll make&nbsp;&nbsp; any threats like that.Frank Ham—five dollars.&nbsp; I have a family friend traveling to Europe next&nbsp;&nbsp; week. It's worried about terror attacks because&nbsp; of the Iran war. Would I tourist travel? I guess&nbsp;&nbsp; I would now—but I'd be more worried in a year than&nbsp; I'd be worried next week. How about you, John?I&nbsp;&nbsp; agree with that. There will always be a threat&nbsp; from lone wolves. And it's true that the Iranians&nbsp;&nbsp; have cells here and there around the world. If&nbsp; you're talking about traveling in Europe—no—I&nbsp;&nbsp; don't think there's any problem. Western audiences&nbsp; always kind of think that there's going to be a&nbsp;&nbsp; quick reaction to this sort of thing. That's not&nbsp; how it is—but there will be a reaction.Thanks&nbsp;&nbsp; for the two-dollar tip on Rumble Wallet. We are&nbsp; number eight in the nation right now on Rumble.&nbsp;&nbsp; I think we have like over 2,000 live people&nbsp; watching live on YouTube. So thank you to all&nbsp;&nbsp; you guys.Will the Kurdish people one day achieve&nbsp; independence and full freedom? Mohammed wants to&nbsp;&nbsp; know.I believe so. I sure hope so. You and I both&nbsp; love the Kurds. You and I both support Kurdish&nbsp;&nbsp; independence. Absolutely. I don't see it happening&nbsp; overnight. But I sure hope someday the Kurds have&nbsp;&nbsp; their independence. They deserve it. It's been&nbsp; promised to them by the biggest powers—and they&nbsp;&nbsp; need it.Someone's asking—please try to answer.&nbsp; Just Samantha—channel activity. Please answer&nbsp;&nbsp; my Super Chat question. Robbie—try to find&nbsp; Samantha's question, please. Things are moving&nbsp;&nbsp; fast.Meow Meow wants to know—is this happening&nbsp; because Benjamin Netanyahu is up for election&nbsp;&nbsp; this year?It's not not a reason. And the ongoing&nbsp; felony corruption charges pending against him. So&nbsp;&nbsp; long as he's the prime minister—he's not going to&nbsp; prison.Why would the State Department list Egypt&nbsp;&nbsp; as somewhere Americans should immediately depart&nbsp; from? Wouldn't be dumb enough to strike at Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp; favorite dictator, right?No—that's not it at all.&nbsp; It's that the Arab street really doesn't like&nbsp;&nbsp; Americans. And so—while we're buddy-buddy with&nbsp; the military dictatorship that rules Egypt—the&nbsp;&nbsp; average Egyptian is pretty darn angry at us right&nbsp; now. No doubt.Tammy—thanks for the question. We'd&nbsp;&nbsp; like to know our opinion on Ayatollah Khamenei&nbsp; as a leader and as a person.Ted—yesterday I did&nbsp;&nbsp; an interview and I stole one of your lines that&nbsp; really went over well.Okay.Killing Khamenei is&nbsp;&nbsp; like killing the Pope. You kill the Pope—you&nbsp; don't bring down Catholicism. They just pick&nbsp;&nbsp; another Pope. Well—killing Khamenei is not going&nbsp; to bring down Iran or Shiism. They're just going&nbsp;&nbsp; to pick another grand ayatollah. And he may be&nbsp; worse.Yeah—that is a hundred percent right.Do we&nbsp;&nbsp; think Trump had the foresight to seize control&nbsp; of Venezuela's oil before destabilizing the&nbsp;&nbsp; Middle East?Yes. I believe that is indeed the&nbsp; case.That's crazy. Just in case we screw things&nbsp;&nbsp; up and the Straits of Hormuz are closed—which they&nbsp; could be closed in the next eight hours.Hello from&nbsp;&nbsp; Vietnam. Thank you for the show. Saw the notice&nbsp; from the State Department for Americans to get&nbsp;&nbsp; out of the Middle East by any means necessary.&nbsp; How should average Americans try to get out in&nbsp;&nbsp; such a situation? The economic fallout is going&nbsp; to be massive. Which dominoes do we see falling&nbsp;&nbsp; first and how will it affect the rest of the&nbsp; globe?I have a lot of experience escaping places&nbsp;&nbsp; that are going tits up. First of all—I like land&nbsp; crossings. Land crossings are better as a rule.&nbsp;&nbsp; But if you're not sure—travel with locals. That's&nbsp; one of my big pieces of advice. Offer—if you know&nbsp;&nbsp; some locals who want to go somewhere—go with&nbsp; them in their shitty local vehicle. That's how&nbsp;&nbsp; I got out of Afghanistan. I was all wrapped up in&nbsp; scarves in the back of an old Soviet pickup truck.&nbsp;&nbsp; We offered the locals—hey—you want to go? Free&nbsp; ride. Come on.But also—you might want to hunker&nbsp;&nbsp; down. I think you're more of a target when you're&nbsp; in motion.Yeah. That's a good point.Which economic&nbsp;&nbsp; dominoes will fall first? I don't know. Trump's&nbsp; chances in the midterm elections?Yeah—we should&nbsp;&nbsp; talk about that actually—if only one third of&nbsp; Republicans think this is a good idea—and if&nbsp;&nbsp; this thing extends beyond the next three&nbsp; or four weeks—we're potentially looking&nbsp;&nbsp; at an anti-Republican bloodbath coming in&nbsp; November.John—I wrote my column this week&nbsp;&nbsp; about the possibility of a canceled election.&nbsp; But I'm not alone now in my paranoia. The&nbsp;&nbsp; Atlantic and NPR both are entertaining the same&nbsp; possibility.Yes—that is true. It's never happened&nbsp;&nbsp; before in American history. But elections have&nbsp; been delayed. The New York mayoral race was&nbsp;&nbsp; delayed after 9/11. And it changed the outcome and&nbsp; Mike Bloomberg won.Yeah—it would have been Mark&nbsp;&nbsp; Green otherwise. Mark Green was great. He would&nbsp; have been a fantastic mayor of New York. He was&nbsp;&nbsp; consumer advocate for New York City. Among other&nbsp; things he said women get overcharged for haircuts.&nbsp;&nbsp; Why should women get charged more for haircuts&nbsp; than men? So he passed a law to make it the same.&nbsp;&nbsp; And he said why should a woman's blouse cost more&nbsp; to dry clean than a man's shirt? It's the same&nbsp;&nbsp; exact object. But then all that was reversed under&nbsp; Bloomberg.Kieran—thanks for the five pounds. We&nbsp;&nbsp; see the BBC is biased in the UK—but in the US it&nbsp; seems less biased. I find that crazy how bad the&nbsp;&nbsp; propaganda is in the US.My take on that, John—is&nbsp; that the BBC is less trustworthy about things in&nbsp;&nbsp; the UK. But the BBC is less biased about the&nbsp; U.S. The BBC has a long history of offering&nbsp;&nbsp; both sides—which we don't do here in the United&nbsp; States.When I was in the CIA and we were getting&nbsp;&nbsp; ready to liberate Kuwait—it would drive me crazy&nbsp; to watch the BBC and they say the Iraqis invaded&nbsp;&nbsp; Kuwait—but the poor starving Iraqi children—the&nbsp; sanctions are killing them. But food's not&nbsp;&nbsp; sanctioned. It's just that Saddam won't distribute&nbsp; the food. That drove me nuts.Sudan—thanks.&nbsp;&nbsp; Speaking of Saddam—Sudan—with just a minute or&nbsp; two left. Thanks for the 49.99. Very generous.&nbsp;&nbsp; Thank you. Saddam and Qadhafi—the buffer for us&nbsp; with Iran?Yes. They were the counterbalance.The&nbsp;&nbsp; user—thanks for the 219 Turkish lira. What's the&nbsp; end game for Israel? No country active besides&nbsp;&nbsp; Turkey in terms of what to add in the region.&nbsp; Europe is asleep and other Gulf countries are just&nbsp;&nbsp; not as effective.Israel is on the [ __ ] warpath,&nbsp; man. I don't know. We're the only ones who can&nbsp;&nbsp; tell them stop and we just won't do it.No—that's&nbsp; what's needed. We need to elect a president who&nbsp;&nbsp; would just cut them off.We're at nine fifty-nine.&nbsp; I am very apologetic. There was just no way to get&nbsp;&nbsp; to all of your questions—many of which were really&nbsp; great and deserved to be answered today.But the&nbsp;&nbsp; good news is we're back tomorrow and Thursday&nbsp; and Friday and we will have Aquiles Larrea with&nbsp;&nbsp; us at 9:15 Eastern time tomorrow. We're here at 9&nbsp; a.m. Eastern Monday through Friday. We'll be back&nbsp;&nbsp; tomorrow at 9.Thanks for tuning in. Please like,&nbsp; follow, and share the show. Thank you once again&nbsp;&nbsp; for your generous support of Robby aid—allowing&nbsp; us to do this show together.John—always great&nbsp;&nbsp; to see you. Please take it for the TMI show with&nbsp; me and Manila Chan. That's coming up right now.


